
{"id":"bd2512c3-2544-484b-94ed-0967ff1907ab","object":"chat.completion","created":1784821530,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    for key in ('min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent'):\\n        if key not in payload:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {key}\\\")\\n    min_size = payload['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters_data = payload['clusters']\\n    parent_map = payload['parent']\\n    if not isinstance(min_size, int) or not isinstance(clusters_data, list) or not isinstance(parent_map, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid types for fields\\\")\\n    cluster_dict = {}\\n    for c in clusters_data:\\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid cluster_id\\\")\\n        size = c.get('size')\\n        birth = c.get('birth_lambda')\\n        exits = c.get('point_exit_lambdas')\\n        if not isinstance(size, int) or not isinstance(birth, (int, float)) or not isinstance(exits, list):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid cluster fields\\\")\\n        if not all(isinstance(e, (int, float)) for e in exits):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid exit lambda\\\")\\n        cluster_dict[cid] = {'size': size, 'birth_lambda': birth, 'point_exit_lambdas': exits}\\n    for cid in parent_map:\\n        if cid not in cluster_dict:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent map key {cid} not in clusters\\\")\\n        par = parent_map[cid]\\n        if par is not None and par not in cluster_dict:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent {par} not in clusters\\\")\\n    retained_ids = {cid for cid, data in cluster_dict.items() if data['size'] >= min_size}\\n    parent_retained = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        cur = parent_map.get(cid)\\n        while cur is not None and cur not in retained_ids:\\n            cur = parent_map.get(cur)\\n        parent_retained[cid] = cur\\n    children_retained = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        par = parent_retained[cid]\\n        if par is not None:\\n            children_retained[par].append(cid)\\n    stability_map = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        data = cluster_dict[cid]\\n        birth = data['birth_lambda']\\n        total = 0.0\\n        for ex in data['point_exit_lambdas']:\\n            diff = ex - birth\\n            if diff > 0:\\n                total += diff\\n        stability_map[cid] = total\\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda cid: (-stability_map[cid], cid))\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    def get_descendants(node):\\n        desc = set()\\n        stack = list(children_retained.get(node, []))\\n        while stack:\\n            child = stack.pop()\\n            if child in desc:\\n                continue\\n            desc.add(child)\\n            stack.extend(children_retained.get(child, []))\\n        return desc\\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        descendants = get_descendants(cid)\\n        if descendants & selected:\\n            continue\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        cur = parent_retained.get(cid)\\n        while cur is not None:\\n            blocked.add(cur)\\n            cur = parent_retained.get(cur)\\n    clusters_out = []\\n    for cid in sorted(retained_ids):\\n        clusters_out.append({\\\"cluster_id\\\": cid, \\\"stability\\\": stability_map[cid]})\\n    selected_ids_out = sorted(selected)\\n    return {\\\"clusters\\\": clusters_out, \\\"selected_ids\\\": selected_ids_out}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that processes the given payload according to the description. The task is \"DATA-HDB-02\" which appears to be about condensed tree stability selection. The input includes clusters with size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas, parent relationships, and min_cluster_size. We need to:\n\n- Validate required fields (present, types, etc.)\n- Drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n- For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum over its points of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). However, note: point_exit_lambdas list may have length less than size? Actually size is the total number of points in the cluster. The point_exit_lambdas list likely gives exit lambdas for points that have exited? The description says \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" But the payload gives point_exit_lambdas as a list. Possibly each point has a birth_lambda (same for all points in the cluster? Actually birth_lambda is per cluster) and each point has its own exit_lambda. The length of point_exit_lambdas might be less than size? For c0 size=7, point_exit_lambdas length=3. That suggests only some points have exited? Or maybe the list gives the exit lambdas for the points that have exited, and the rest are still alive? But the stability formula sums over points. Need to interpret.\n\nThe typical HDBSCAN condensed tree: each cluster has a birth level (lambda) and each point has an exit level (lambda when it leaves the cluster). The cluster stability is sum over points of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for points that exit after birth (i.e., positive). Points that die before birth? Not relevant. So we need the exit_lambda for every point in the cluster. The payload gives point_exit_lambdas. For c0 size=7, only 3 values. That implies the other points have no recorded exit? Maybe they are still in the cluster at the end? In HDBSCAN, the condensed tree includes only points that exit, but each cluster's points are those that are members at that level? Actually the condensed tree structure: each cluster node has a list of points that die (exit) at that node. The size is the total number of points that belong to the cluster (including those that exit at that node or are passed to children). The point_exit_lambdas are the exit lambdas for those points that exit at this cluster (i.e., die in this cluster). The other points are passed to child clusters. So the stability for a cluster is sum over the points that exit in this cluster of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda). And also stability contributions from children? No, cluster stability is defined per node as sum of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth) for points that die at that node. The total stability of a cluster in a flat selection considers the subtree stability? Actually HDBSCAN's stability-based selection: for each node, its stability = sum over its own points (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for points that die at that node. Then for selection, we compare node stability against sum of stabilities of its descendants. The algorithm: if node's stability > sum of descendant stabilities, select node, else propagate stability up.\n\nBut the description says: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" That is the per-cluster stability. Then \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" and \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This sounds like a greedy selection process based on stability, not the full HDBSCAN propagation. However the mention of \"descendant blocks it\" and \"block its ancestors\" suggests a tree-based selection: you process clusters in decreasing stability order, and if a cluster's descendant is already selected, you cannot select it; if you select a cluster, you block all its ancestors (so they cannot be selected later). That is a common approach for condensed tree selection to avoid overlapping clusters.\n\nGiven the input: clusters have parent relationships forming a tree (parent: cluster_id -> parent_id or null for root). We need to build the tree, drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n\nBut careful: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" This likely means we should prune the tree: remove clusters whose size < min_cluster_size. However, if a parent is dropped, its children should still be considered? Typically in condensed tree, the tree already has sizes; we ignore clusters below min size. But need to handle parent references: if a cluster's parent is dropped, then its parent becomes the parent of the dropped cluster? Or we treat the dropped cluster as non-existent? The description: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" After dropping, we have a set of retained clusters. Then we compute stability for each retained cluster. Then we need to consider parent relationships among retained clusters? The tree might be broken. Probably we should only consider the retained clusters as nodes, and their parent relationships if the parent is also retained. If parent not retained, then that cluster becomes a root? But the description says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So ancestors must be in the retained set. If a parent is dropped, the child's ancestry may skip. To handle this, we should reconstruct the tree among retained clusters: for each retained cluster, find the nearest retained ancestor (i.e., the first ancestor that is also retained). That would be the effective parent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size\" means we simply ignore them when computing stability? But the selection process needs the tree structure. The example: c2 size=2, min_cluster_size=3, so c2 is dropped. c0 and c1 are retained. Parent of c1 is c0 (retained). c2 parent is c0, but c2 dropped, so c0's descendants include c1 only. So the tree is fine.\n\nSo algorithm:\n1. Validate payload: must have min_cluster_size (int >=0?), clusters (list), parent (dict mapping cluster_id to parent_id or None). Each cluster must have cluster_id, size (int), birth_lambda (float), point_exit_lambdas (list of float). The lengths of point_exit_lambdas might be less than or equal to size? Actually for condensed tree, the list gives exit lambdas for points that die at this node. So the number of exit lambdas can be less than size. We'll assume that's the case. We need to compute stability as sum over point_exit_lambdas of max(0, exit - birth_lambda). But the description says \"over its points\". Could include points that die in children? No, stability for cluster node is only its own points. So we'll compute that.\n\n2. Validate: all cluster_ids present in clusters list, parent keys match cluster_ids, parent values either null or existing cluster_id. Check for cycles? Could do simple validation.\n\n3. Build dict: cluster_id -> {size, birth_lambda, exit_lambdas, children list}. We'll also maintain original order for tie-breaking.\n\n4. Drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size. Remove them from the dict and from parent references. For retained clusters, we need to recompute parent to nearest retained ancestor. We can do: for each cluster, go up parent chain until we find a retained parent or null. If null, it's a root.\n\n5. For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum(max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for exit_lambda in point_exit_lambdas). Note: if point_exit_lambdas is empty, stability = 0.\n\n6. Build list of (cluster_id, stability, children list) for retained clusters. Also keep track of original cluster_id for tie-breaking.\n\n7. Rank: sort by decreasing stability, then cluster_id (probably lexicographic? Since cluster IDs appear to be strings like \"c0\", \"c1\". \"c2\" is dropped. So by cluster_id string. The description says \"cluster ID\". We'll use string comparison.\n\n8. Selection: Initialize selected set empty, blocked ancestors set? Actually \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That means we process clusters in the ranked order. For each cluster, check if any of its descendants (in the retained tree) is already selected. If yes, skip (it is blocked). If no, select it, and then block all its ancestors (i.e., mark them as such that they cannot be selected later). But blocking ancestors means those ancestors are not selectable even if they come later in the order. However, the algorithm says \"block its ancestors\". So after selecting, we mark all ancestors as blocked. And if a cluster is blocked, skip it. Also, if a cluster is a descendant of a selected cluster, it is also blocked? Actually \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means you cannot select a cluster that has a descendant already selected (because that would create overlapping clusters? The selection aims to pick clusters that are not nested). If you select a cluster, you block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected). But what about descendants of the selected cluster? They are already blocked because they are descendants of a selected cluster, so they will be skipped when they come later due to \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" condition? Actually if you select a cluster, its descendants are not yet selected (they come later perhaps), but when later processing a descendant, it will check if any selected descendant blocks it? The descendant's descendants are not selected, but the selected cluster is its ancestor, not descendant. The condition only checks descendants, not ancestors. So after selecting a cluster, its descendants are NOT blocked by the condition. But they should be blocked because you don't want to select a child of a selected cluster. The typical algorithm: in stability-based selection, you compare node stability vs descendant stabilities, but here they use a greedy top-down? Actually the description: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That suggests we process from highest stability down, and if we select a cluster, we block its ancestors. But what about its descendants? They are not blocked by the condition unless they have a selected descendant. But if we select a cluster, its descendants are not selected yet. However, when later processing a descendant, it will check if any selected descendant blocks it. Its only selected descendant? None? But it has a selected ancestor, but the condition only checks descendants. So that descendant would not be blocked unless it has a selected descendant (which it doesn't). So you could end up selecting both a parent and a child, which contradicts the idea of non-overlapping selection. Perhaps the condition is \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" meaning if any descendant is selected, you cannot select this cluster. But if you select a cluster, its ancestors are blocked, so when later processing an ancestor, it will see a selected descendant (the original cluster) and thus be blocked. That part works. But for descendants of the selected cluster, they have no selected descendants (unless they have their own selected children), so they could be selected later, creating overlapping clusters. That's undesirable.\n\nMaybe the correct interpretation: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if any descendant is already selected, this cluster is blocked. And \"then block its ancestors\" means after selecting, we mark all ancestors as blocked (so they cannot be selected later). That prevents ancestors from being selected, but does not prevent descendants from being selected. However, if a descendant is selected later, that would create a situation where an ancestor and descendant are both selected, which violates the no-overlap? Actually if we select a descendant, it will have an ancestor that is already selected, but the condition for selecting that descendant checks \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". It has no selected descendant (since it is a leaf maybe), so it could be selected. But then both are selected, which is wrong because they are nested. The typical condensed tree selection selects either a node or its descendants, but not both. So the condition must prevent selecting a node that has a selected ancestor as well? Or the selection process should ensure that once a node is selected, all its descendants are automatically blocked (or they cannot be selected because they would have a selected ancestor, but the condition doesn't check ancestors). This is ambiguous.\n\nLet's re-read the description: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That suggests that selection proceeds in descending stability order. If we select a cluster, we block its ancestors (so they can't be selected later). But descendants are not blocked. However, if later a descendant is considered, it will check if any already selected descendant blocks it. Since its descendants are not selected (unless there are deeper), it might be selected. So both could be selected. That seems against the idea of a flat clustering.\n\nMaybe the condition also means \"no already selected descendant\" includes the case where the cluster itself is a descendant of an already selected cluster? Actually \"descendant\" of this cluster? The phrasing \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if any descendant of this cluster is already selected, then this cluster is blocked (cannot be selected). That is what I understood. So after selecting a cluster, its ancestors are blocked, but its descendants are not automatically blocked because they are not ancestors. But the descendant, when later processed, will check its own descendants. It might find none selected, so it could be selected. That would cause both to be selected. However, note that if we select a descendant later, that descendant will block its ancestors (including the already selected cluster) but ancestors are already blocked anyway. The problem is that both are selected, which might be acceptable if the definition of \"blocks\" only prevents selection of ancestors, not descendants. But then we would have overlapping clusters.\n\nMaybe the intended algorithm is: process in decreasing stability. For each cluster, if none of its descendants in the retained tree have been selected yet, select it and mark all its ancestors as blocked (i.e., cannot be selected later). But also, if a cluster is descendant of a selected cluster, it will be blocked because its ancestor is selected? But the condition only checks descendants. So we need to also block descendants of selected clusters. The typical way: after selecting a cluster, mark it and all its descendants as \"selected\" or \"blocked\"? Actually in HDBSCAN, the selection algorithm works bottom-up: compute for each node the \"sol\" (something) and decide to select node if its stability > sum of descendant stabilities, then propagate. That yields a set of nodes that are not ancestors/descendants of each other.\n\nBut the description here says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" and \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That suggests a greedy top-down approach. The phrase \"block its ancestors\" means that if you select a cluster, you prevent any of its ancestors from being selected later. But what about its descendants? They are not blocked by this action. However, if you later consider a descendant, it will check its own descendants. If none, it passes and can be selected. This would allow both a parent and a child to be selected if the parent has higher stability. That seems odd.\n\nLet's look at the example fixtures: there are three cases, all identical payload. The expected output is not given, but we can infer from the task. Since no expected output is provided, we need to implement a reasonable algorithm that matches the description.\n\nI suspect the intended algorithm is: For each retained cluster, compute its stability. Then perform a greedy selection: sort clusters by stability descending, then cluster ID. Maintain a set of \"blocked\" clusters (cannot be selected). Initially empty. For each cluster in sorted order, if it is not blocked, select it, and then add all its ancestors (up the retained tree) to the blocked set. Also, what about its descendants? They are not blocked by this selection, but if a descendant comes later, it will not be blocked unless it has a selected descendant (which it doesn't). But if we select a cluster, we should also block its descendants from being selected later because they are nested. The description only says \"block its ancestors\", not descendants. However, \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if a descendant is already selected, then this cluster is blocked. That handles the reverse: if a child is selected first, then the parent is blocked. So the selection process ensures that no selected cluster is an ancestor of another selected cluster. But it does not prevent a selected cluster from being a descendant of another selected cluster if the ancestor was selected first. That would be a violation. To prevent that, when we select a cluster, we must block all its descendants as well. But the description says only ancestors. Perhaps \"block its ancestors\" means once a cluster is selected, all clusters that are ancestors of it are blocked (cannot be selected). That prevents ancestors from being selected later. But it does not prevent descendants. So if a high-stability ancestor is selected first, its descendants are still eligible and may be selected later if they have no selected descendants. That would create nested selections. That seems wrong.\n\nMaybe the order ensures that if an ancestor is selected first, its descendants will have a selected ancestor, but the condition only checks descendants, not ancestors. So they would be allowed. That seems like a bug. Perhaps the condition should also check that no ancestor is selected? Or the selection process should mark all nodes in the subtree as blocked? Given the description \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" and \"then block its ancestors\", it might be that the algorithm processes from leaves upward? But it says \"decreasing stability\". Leaves might have lower stability.\n\nLet's think about the typical condensed tree selection: you compute stability for each node. Then you perform a bottom-up traversal: for each node, if node stability > sum of child stabilities, select the node and set its stability to node stability (so parent uses this value). Otherwise, do not select the node and set its stability to sum of child stabilities. At the end, selected nodes are those that were selected in that process. That yields a set of non-overlapping clusters. That algorithm uses descendant stability sums, not just descendant selection.\n\nBut the description explicitly says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That is different. It's a greedy algorithm based on individual cluster stability, not considering children's stability.\n\nGiven the ambiguity and the fact that no example output is given, I'll implement a straightforward interpretation that seems plausible and matches the words:\n\n- Build retained tree.\n- Compute stability for each retained cluster as sum of (max(0, exit - birth)) for its points.\n- Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id).\n- Iterate in that order. Maintain a set `selected` and a set `blocked`. For each cluster:\n  - Check if any descendant of this cluster (in retained tree) is already selected. If yes, skip.\n  - Else, select this cluster: add to selected, and add all ancestors of this cluster (including itself? Usually \"block its ancestors\" means ancestors, not itself) to blocked set. Also, we should probably add all descendants? The description says only ancestors. But to prevent future descendants from being selected, maybe we also need to block descendants. However, the condition \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" will catch descendants only if they are selected before the parent. If the parent is selected first, descendants are not blocked. So to avoid overlap, we must block descendants as well. I'll assume that \"block its ancestors\" is a misstatement and we should block all clusters that are in its lineage (both ancestors and descendants) to prevent any overlap. But the description explicitly says \"ancestors\". Given the strict instruction to treat properties as hard contracts, I'll follow the literal: only block ancestors. Then the selection may produce nested clusters. That could be acceptable as output? Unlikely.\n\nLet's examine the phrase \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". This phrasing implies that we check if any descendant is already selected. If an ancestor is selected first, then when we later consider a descendant, it will check its descendants (none) so it will be selected. That leads to nested selections. To prevent that, we need to also block descendants when selecting an ancestor. So maybe the intended algorithm is to process clusters in decreasing stability, and for each, if neither it nor any of its ancestors/descendants have been selected, select it and block all ancestors and descendants. But they only mention ancestors. Could be a simplification.\n\nAnother approach: implement the standard HDBSCAN stability selection (bottom-up comparison) as it is common and likely intended. But the description says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID\" and \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" which is different.\n\nGiven the strict nature, I'll implement the literal interpretation: sort by stability desc, then cluster ID asc. For each cluster in that order, if none of its descendants (in the retained tree) are selected, select it and block all its ancestors (mark them as blocked). Additionally, after selecting, we should also block the cluster itself? It says \"block its ancestors\", not itself, but if we don't block itself, it could be considered again? No, we only process each once. So we select it and then for each ancestor up to root, mark them as blocked. Then later when we encounter an ancestor, we check if any descendant selected blocks it. If the ancestor has the selected cluster as a descendant, then it will be blocked. So that works. For a descendant of the selected cluster, it will not be blocked because its ancestor is selected but condition only checks descendants. So that descendant could be selected. That yields nested selections. But maybe that's the desired behavior? Unlikely.\n\nGiven the task is \"DATA-HDB-02\" and it's about condensed tree stability, likely they want the standard bottom-up selection. However, I'll stick to the literal description because the prompt says \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Condensed-tree construction, cluster stability, and flat selection; metric learning is excluded.\" and \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences\". The description of selection is explicit. I'll implement that.\n\nThus, steps after building retained tree:\n\n- Compute stability for each cluster (sum over its point_exit_lambdas of max(0, exit - birth_lambda)).\n- Build list of (stability, cluster_id, cluster) for retained clusters.\n- Sort by (-stability, cluster_id).\n- selected = set()\n- blocked = set()\n- For each cluster in sorted order:\n  if cluster_id in blocked: continue\n  # Check if any descendant is selected\n  descendants_set = set of all descendants of this cluster in retained tree.\n  if descendants_set & selected:\n      continue  # blocked by descendant\n  # Else select\n  selected.add(cluster_id)\n  # Block ancestors\n  cur = cluster_id\n  while True:\n      parent = parent_map_retained[cur]  # nearest retained parent or None\n      if parent is None:\n          break\n      blocked.add(parent)\n      cur = parent\n- After loop, result: clusters_stabilities: list of dicts? The output should be \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" That suggests we need to return a JSON object containing:\n  - For each retained cluster: cluster_id, stability, size, maybe birth_lambda? \"stability rows\" likely meaning list of objects with cluster_id and stability.\n  - \"sorted selected IDs\": list of selected cluster_ids sorted by cluster ID? Or sorted by stability? The description: \"sorted selected IDs\" - likely sorted by cluster ID (ascending) to have deterministic output.\n- Also need to include \"parent\" field? The parent relationships might be needed for audit? The description: \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\". We could include the parent map in the output? But not explicitly required. The output should be JSON-serializable. I think the expected output is something like:\n{\n  \"clusters\": [  // retained clusters with stability\n    {\"cluster_id\": \"c0\", \"stability\": ..., \"size\": ...},\n    {\"cluster_id\": \"c1\", \"stability\": ..., \"size\": ...}\n  ],\n  \"selected_ids\": [\"c1\", ...]  // sorted\n}\nBut we need to decide exact fields. The prompt says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So likely the result is a dict with keys: \"stabilities\" (list of objects with cluster_id and stability) and \"selected_ids\" (list). Also maybe \"cluster_ids\" or something. I'll go with \"clusters\" as list of dicts with cluster_id, stability, and \"selected_ids\" list. Also include \"min_cluster_size\" for completeness? Not needed.\n\nAlso, we need to raise ValueError for invalid cases.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Validate: payload must have min_cluster_size, clusters, parent. clusters must be list, parent dict. Each cluster must have cluster_id, size (int), birth_lambda (float), point_exit_lambdas (list of floats). Also check that parent keys match cluster_ids, and parent values are either None or existent cluster_id. Check for cycles? Could do simple DFS.\n\n- Build cluster_dict: key cluster_id, store all data.\n\n- Filter retained: size >= min_cluster_size.\n\n- Rebuild parent mapping for retained: for each retained cluster, follow parent chain in original dict until we find a retained cluster or None. If None, parent is None. This gives parent_retained dict.\n\n- Build tree: for each retained cluster, add to children list of parent_retained.\n\n- Compute stability: sum(max(0, exit - birth_lambda) for exit in point_exit_lambdas). Note: the birth_lambda is per cluster, same for all its points.\n\n- Sort retained clusters by stability desc, then cluster_id asc.\n\n- Selection as described.\n\n- Prepare result: list of cluster dicts for retained clusters (including cluster_id, stability, size, birth_lambda maybe) but only stability and cluster_id are needed for stability rows. I'll include cluster_id and stability. Also include size for completeness? Not required but safe.\n\n- selected_ids sorted by cluster_id ascending (since they said \"sorted selected IDs\").\n\nReturn dict.\n\nEdge cases: What if no retained clusters? Return empty list for clusters and empty list for selected_ids.\n\nAlso need to handle if min_cluster_size is <= 0? Possibly but assume positive.\n\nNote: The payload includes \"_case_nonce\" which is not used.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that takes case dict, extracts payload (case['payload']), validates, and returns result.\n\nNow write the JSON output: entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string (escaped). So we need to produce a JSON string with that structure.\n\nI'll write the code in a string, then escape it for JSON.\n\nLet's write the Python code first:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    # Validate required fields\n    for key in ('min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent'):\n        if key not in payload:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing required field: {key}\")\n    min_size = payload['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_data = payload['clusters']\n    parent_map = payload['parent']\n    if not isinstance(min_size, int) or not isinstance(clusters_data, list) or not isinstance(parent_map, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid types for fields\")\n    # Build cluster dict\n    cluster_dict = {}\n    for c in clusters_data:\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid cluster_id\")\n        size = c.get('size')\n        birth = c.get('birth_lambda')\n        exits = c.get('point_exit_lambdas')\n        if not isinstance(size, int) or not isinstance(birth, (int, float)) or not isinstance(exits, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid cluster fields\")\n        if not all(isinstance(e, (int, float)) for e in exits):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid exit lambda\")\n        cluster_dict[cid] = {'size': size, 'birth_lambda': birth, 'point_exit_lambdas': exits}\n\n    # Validate parent map keys\n    for cid in parent_map:\n        if cid not in cluster_dict:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Parent map key {cid} not in clusters\")\n        par = parent_map[cid]\n        if par is not None and par not in cluster_dict:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Parent {par} not in clusters\")\n    # Check for cycles? Not mandatory but we can do simple depth check.\n    # We'll trust input is a tree.\n\n    # Retained clusters\n    retained_ids = {cid for cid, data in cluster_dict.items() if data['size'] >= min_size}\n\n    # Build parent_retained mapping\n    parent_retained = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        cur = parent_map.get(cid)\n        while cur is not None and cur not in retained_ids:\n            cur = parent_map.get(cur)\n        parent_retained[cid] = cur  # None if no retained ancestor\n\n    # Build children list for each retained cluster\n    children_retained = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        par = parent_retained[cid]\n        if par is not None:\n            children_retained[par].append(cid)\n\n    # Compute stability\n    stability_map = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        data = cluster_dict[cid]\n        birth = data['birth_lambda']\n        total = 0.0\n        for ex in data['point_exit_lambdas']:\n            diff = ex - birth\n            if diff > 0:\n                total += diff\n        stability_map[cid] = total\n\n    # Sort retained clusters\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda cid: (-stability_map[cid], cid))\n\n    # Selection\n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n    # Helper to get all descendants (DFS)\n    def get_descendants(node):\n        desc = set()\n        stack = list(children_retained.get(node, []))\n        while stack:\n            child = stack.pop()\n            if child in desc:\n                continue\n            desc.add(child)\n            stack.extend(children_retained.get(child, []))\n        return desc\n\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        descendants = get_descendants(cid)\n        if descendants & selected:\n            continue\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # Block ancestors\n        cur = parent_retained.get(cid)\n        while cur is not None:\n            blocked.add(cur)\n            cur = parent_retained.get(cur)\n\n    # Prepare output\n    clusters_out = []\n    for cid in sorted(retained_ids):  # sort by cluster_id for deterministic order? We'll use original order within the loop\n        clusters_out.append({\"cluster_id\": cid, \"stability\": stability_map[cid]})\n    # Alternatively, use the order of sorted_clusters? But \"stability rows\" likely sorted by something. We'll use cluster_id order.\n    selected_ids_out = sorted(selected)\n    return {\"clusters\": clusters_out, \"selected_ids\": selected_ids_out}\n```\n\nBut we need to ensure we don't mutate case; w